{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>You shall read this Torah opposite all Israel, in their ears.</b> Now, anyone who reads to his fellow in his ear does not stand opposite him but rather at his side. And perhaps from here our Rabbis learned (Sotah 41a): on the night after the first day of the festival of Sukkot they make for the king a wooden platform in the Courtyard, etc., and he reads beginning from ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים״ and onward. And from where did they learn to say thus, that he begins from ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים״? For it would seem more fitting to say that he should begin from ״הוֹאִיל מֹשֶׁה בֵּאֵר אֶת הַתּוֹרָה הַזֹּאת.״ Rather, they learned this from what is written ״נֶגֶד כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ for every verse of ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים״ stood opposite all Israel, for he would allude to all the places where they provoked the Holy One, blessed be He, yet for the honor of Israel he did so only by hint. And a matter spoken by hint is like whispering in one's ear — those who hear do not understand anything, except for one who feels within himself that he sinned in that matter; he alone understands that allusion. And concerning this it says that he shall read this Torah — which is ״נֶגֶד כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and ״בְּאָזְנֵיהֶם,״ meaning the Torah that stands opposite Israel and is spoken in their ears by way of hint. And there it says: ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר מֹשֶׁה אֶל כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל;״ therefore it says here ״נֶגֶד כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ for at this assembly it is impossible for all Israel throughout the entire land to be present, but rather he is speaking of the utterance of Moses, which took place with all Israel standing." "And from here there is a lesson for all words of reproof regarding sin: the one who rebukes must speak by way of hint concerning the sin in which a particular individual has transgressed, so that the matter enters only into his ears and others do not understand it, for the sake of his honor — in the manner that Moses did."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The Talmudic passage cited (Sotah 41a) describes the king's reading at the Hakhel assembly; the Kli Yakar uses it to explain why the reading begins specifically from Deuteronomy 1:1 (אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים) rather than from Deuteronomy 1:5 (הוֹאִיל מֹשֶׁה)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:18.760136418Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}